    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d861">

                <pb facs="0794" n="678" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg678"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 882.51/361.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the American
                        Minister</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 27.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-05-15">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 15, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to Mr. Reed Paige Clark’s cable
                    from London of April 5 and the Department’s telegraphic reply of April 8, 1912,
                    upon the subject of the designation of the three Receivers to the different
                    customs posts in Liberia, I transmit herewith for the confidential information
                    of your Legation and Mr. Clark, duplicate copies of the correspondence on this
                    subject recently exchanged by the Department with the German and French
                    Ambassadors at this capital.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d861-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Huntington Wilson.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d861-f1" n="2" xml:id="d861-f1-content">
                <p>All the inclosures with this instruction are printed, ante, in their
                    chronological order; they are: The note from the French Ambassador, March 30;
                    the memorandum from the German Ambassador, April 22; the Department’s reply to
                    the French Ambassador, May 15; the Department’s reply to the German Ambassador,
                    May 15.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
